WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:01.163
- [Instructor] In this video

00:00:01.163 --> 00:00:04.720
we're gonna familiarize
ourselves with matter.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.450
We're gonna thinking about
what matters about matter

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:09.210
but in particular, we're gonna
think about a lot of words

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:11.000
you'll hear, especially
in a chemistry class

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.770
and how they might relate to each other

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:14.640
when we're classifying matter.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.400
So first let's start with
a definition of matter.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.654
Well, a good definition
is anything that has mass

00:00:20.654 --> 00:00:22.530
and anything that has volume

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:24.887
where it takes up
three-dimensional space is matter

00:00:24.887 --> 00:00:27.420
and it has to do both of these things.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.740
Mass, you probably have
an intuitive sense.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:32.100
An elephant has more mass than a mouse.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:33.530
If we wanna get a little more technical

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:37.440
it's related to how hard it
is to accelerate something.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:38.273
Now you might be saying,

00:00:38.273 --> 00:00:40.720
"Isn't this everything
that has mass and volume?"

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:41.980
But we have to remind ourselves

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:43.580
even within scientific circles,

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:44.790
there's many things we talk about

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:47.560
that don't have both mass and volume.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:50.600
For example, we can think about waves.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.323
We could think about energy.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:56.010
And then you can think about
things that have energy

00:00:56.010 --> 00:01:01.010
or that have wave-like
properties like light or sound.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:05.120
And all of these things would
not be considered matter.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:08.070
And of course, I guess you
could even broaden it to ideas

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.370
like love that also would not be matter

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:13.140
but I'm probably going on
a bit of a tangent there

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.360
but we're gonna stay
focused on the matter side

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.400
not on the not matter.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.780
And the first division
of matter is between

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:25.780
what we could view as pure
substances and mixtures.

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:30.860
Now a pure substance, one
way to think about it is

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:33.710
no matter how small we
get any sample we take

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:36.330
and we don't chemically alter that sample,

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:38.270
so we're not doing any chemistry making

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:39.990
or breaking bonds here.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:41.160
If we take a chunk of it

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.420
you're gonna have consistent
chemical properties.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:44.660
You're going to get the same thing.

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:46.580
Once again, assuming

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:49.220
that you're not changing
the structure chemically,

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:51.790
you're not making or
breaking chemical bonds,

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:53.500
you can get down to the microscopic

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:54.980
to even the molecular level

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:57.050
and you're going to have
the same properties.

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:59.420
While in a mixture that isn't true.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:01.340
That if you get close enough

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:02.720
you're gonna get chunks of things

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:04.540
that have different properties.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:07.000
Now within pure substances
and I'll give a little bit

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:08.810
of a division within mixtures as well.

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:11.450
Pure substances can be further subdivided.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.941
So here we could have a pure element.

00:02:13.941 --> 00:02:15.670
So this is a pure substance

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:18.247
that only has one type of element in it.

00:02:18.247 --> 00:02:21.150
And this is an example of pure gold.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:22.520
And in reality

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:26.400
it's hard to have an
absolute 100% pure thing

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.490
but most of these gold
bars as you see here

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:31.620
you're gonna get a gold atom there.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:34.250
If you zoom in enough, it is pure gold.

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:35.940
It is a pure element.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:40.340
Well, what we have over
here, this is pure water.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:42.820
Now water is still a pure substance

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:45.840
because you go down all the
way to the smallest level,

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.120
to a molecular level.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:50.840
As long as you are not
chemically altering the water

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:55.290
you are going to still get
the same thing at any scale.

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:58.520
But water is made up of
different types of elements.

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:00.620
The chemical formula for water,

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:04.390
every water molecule has two
hydrogens and one oxygen.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:06.430
And so when you have a pure substance

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:11.120
that has more than one type of
element in its smallest unit

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:14.813
that has its chemical properties
we call that a compound.

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:18.520
Now I also use this word molecule

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:20.030
when I talked about a water molecule

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:21.270
and sometimes it's confusing.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:22.410
When do you use compound?

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:23.650
When do you use molecule?

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:27.240
And one way to think about
it is a molecule is anything

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:31.400
that has more than one
atom bound together.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:34.240
While a compound is a subset of molecules

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:37.790
that it has different types
of atoms bound together.

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:40.820
So for example, if we're
thinking about molecules

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:45.540
I'm drawing a little bit of
a a little Venn diagram here,

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.400
or a set diagram, I should say.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:50.163
Examples of molecules could
be molecular hydrogen.

00:03:51.326 --> 00:03:52.180
It has to have the same atom

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:53.990
but they are bonded to each other.

00:03:53.990 --> 00:03:56.120
And so actually, if you had a bunch of H2,

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:59.220
molecular hydrogen, that
would be a pure element.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:03.720
It could be O2 molecular
oxygen, that is a molecule.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:05.880
But if we're thinking about compounds,

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:07.130
we could think about H2O.

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:10.740
That is both a molecule and a compound.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:12.120
All compounds are molecules

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:14.520
but not all the molecules are compounds.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.550
If we think about carbon
dioxide, that is a compound.

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:19.890
It has not only more than one atom

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:22.540
but the atoms are of different types.

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:24.640
And of course you can have pure substances

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:27.450
that are neither molecules nor compounds.

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:31.550
You could have a situation
like neon, which is a noble gas

00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:33.977
and it doesn't like to bond
with anything even itself.

00:04:33.977 --> 00:04:36.410
And so it could just be a free atom.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:37.840
So if you had a bunch of neon together

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:38.753
that could be a pure element

00:04:38.753 --> 00:04:43.010
that is neither a molecule nor a compound.

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:45.350
Now let's go on the other
side of this little taxonomy

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:47.150
that we're creating to mixture.

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:49.580
So mixture, if a pure
substance, at any scale

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:51.160
you get down a microscopic scale

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:52.860
and as long as you're
not chemically altering

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:54.430
making and breaking bonds,

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:56.220
you're going to get the same properties.

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:58.200
You're going to get the same stuff.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:01.670
But in a mixture, when you
get down to a small scale

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:03.810
you don't necessarily get the same thing

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:06.500
and that you can actually
get different things

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:08.650
not by doing chemical changes

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:11.020
but by doing physical changes to them.

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:12.550
So this right over here

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:14.440
where you can clearly see the difference.

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:17.440
So there, it looks like in this rock here

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:21.480
part of it is this one type
of glassy looking mineral here

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:24.000
and then you have this other
type of mineral looking here.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:27.270
It's clear that these
minerals are mixed together.

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:28.950
And a mixture where it's clear

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:31.890
that the different parts
are different stuff

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:35.773
we would call this a heterogeneous
mixture, heterogeneous.

00:05:37.818 --> 00:05:41.210
Each of these minerals are
oftentimes pure substances

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:43.210
but you have a bunch of pure
substances mixed together,

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:46.220
but it's pretty easy to
pick out where one is

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:47.053
versus the other.

00:05:47.053 --> 00:05:49.430
And they aren't mixed
together in a uniform way.

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:52.860
And so that's why we call
it a heterogeneous mixture.

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:54.930
Now, in this picture on the right

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:58.220
we have to have two mixtures going on.

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:02.740
We have the air up here and
air itself is a mixture.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:04.870
A lot of you might say,
"Wait, air is matter.

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:06.310
It doesn't feel like anything is there."

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:08.560
Well that's just 'cause you're used to it.

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:10.950
Matter is anything with mass or volume

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:14.660
and air especially the air
molecules for sure have mass

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:18.760
and for sure take up space,
they have volume to it.

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:23.130
Matter can come as a gas,
as a liquid, as a solid

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:24.710
and even as plasma,

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:28.940
those are states of matter,
but that is all matter.

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:30.930
But the reason why the air that we breathe

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:32.750
is 'cause considered a mixture

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:36.050
is that it's full of
nitrogen, it's full of oxygen.

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:37.430
It's full of carbon dioxide.

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:39.800
In fact, it's mostly nitrogen and oxygen.

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:42.450
Those things can be physically
separated from itself.

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:44.090
You can zoom in on the air

00:06:44.090 --> 00:06:48.370
and you can do things to just
pick out a nitrogen molecule

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:52.063
or an oxygen molecule or
a carbon dioxide molecule

00:06:52.063 --> 00:06:55.420
without making or breaking bonds.

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:57.750
But air where you have these
different pure substances

00:06:57.750 --> 00:07:01.120
that are mixed together
really well in a uniform way,

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:04.040
this is known as a homogeneous
mixture, homogeneous

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:09.560
and similarly water that you
might see in say the ocean

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:11.590
is also gonna be a homogeneous mixture

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:14.030
because it doesn't just have H2O in it.

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:16.120
It for sure is gonna have H2O in it

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:19.170
but it's also going to
have sodium chloride

00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:21.320
that is been dissolved in it.

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:26.120
So it'll have sodium ions
and chloride anions in it.

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:27.930
It's gonna probably have other things

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:28.860
that are mixed into it.

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:31.630
It's probably gonna have
molecular oxygen mixed into it

00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:34.210
but it's mixed generally pretty uniformly.

00:07:34.210 --> 00:07:38.040
So that's why we call it
a homogeneous mixture.

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:39.660
So I'll let you go there.

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:42.160
This is giving you the
toolkit for a lot of the words

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:43.700
that you will hear in chemistry class.

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:45.750
And it's useful to be able to know

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:47.603
what these different things mean.

